# FlacronCV — Standing Rules

Live production application with real users. Treat every change accordingly.

1. Read before building. PROJECT_PROGRESS.md and ARCHITECTURE_MAP.md first, then only the files this task touches. Much of what looks unbuilt is already built and tested — check before scoping. Never re-scan the whole codebase.
2. Micro-changes only. One logical change, smallest possible diff. No opportunistic refactoring, renaming, reformatting, or dependency bumps.
3. Propose before you edit. Name the files and why. Wait for approval.
4. Update docs in the same change. Not done until PROJECT_PROGRESS.md (status + Change Log) and, if the shape of things moved, ARCHITECTURE_MAP.md are updated.
5. Run pnpm lint, pnpm type-check, pnpm test before declaring done. Measured suite
   sizes move; do not quote a frozen number. As of Batch J (2026-08-19): api **514**,
   web **300**. CI runs them. Never leave the site broken between changes.
6. Every new user-facing string goes through t(), in all six locales. **Seven** gates
   enforce this: six under `apps/web/src/i18n/` (locale parity, key resolution,
   no-hardcoded-English, untranslated, encoding, allowance-copy) plus
   `apps/web/src/lib/chrome-contrast.test.ts`. Parity alone will not catch a key
   missing from every locale — that has shipped three times.
7. Never commit or print secrets. No keys, tokens, or connection strings in code, docs, comments, or terminal output. Never echo an env var value.
8. Never touch auth, billing, or data shape without explicit approval. These are where a small mistake costs real money or real accounts.
9. Do not weaken a guard to make a test pass. Guards in this repo exist because something broke. If a test fails, the code is wrong.
10. All limits are enforced server-side. Disabling a frontend button is UX, never enforcement.
11. Verify against the running system, not source. Use curl, real DNS lookups, actual logs. Past debugging went long because of confident inference from code that did not match what was deployed.
12. Never point local dev at production. FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST is the only guard; without it firebase-admin falls back to service-account credentials and connects to production. The tell in the log is "Firestore connection verified".
13. Say when you are unsure. A wrong confident answer costs more than a question.